:16,"opponent":"PIT","home":true,"pf":31.0,"pa":28.0,"off_snaps":60,"epa_per_play":0.07786,"pass_rate":0.7,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6087,"def_epa_per_play":0.08041},{"week":17,"opponent":"CAR","home":true,"pf":14.0,"pa":33.0,"off_snaps":51,"epa_per_play":-0.1302,"pass_rate":0.4706,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6,"def_epa_per_play":0.08145}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2018,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6267,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"NE","distance":0.7409,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Josh McDaniels"},{"season":2025,"team":"SEA","distance":0.7725,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Klint Kubiak"},{"season":2023,"team":"SF","distance":0.8302,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"},{"season":2024,"team":"BUF","distance":0.853,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Joe Brady"}],"defense":[{"season":2025,"team":"IND","distance":0.5989,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2023,"team":"LAC","distance":0.667,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2023,"team":"PHI","distance":0.7072,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.711,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.7676,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Saints went 13-3 in 2018 and outscored opponents 504 to 353. The offense carried the team, ranking 3rd in EPA per play while the defense finished 12th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the fewest receivers on the field and the 3rd highest nickel usage in the league.","offense":"The Saints had one of the most efficient offenses in the league, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the fewest receivers on the field and the 2nd highest 22 personnel usage in the league. Drew Brees ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Michael Thomas, who drew 29% of the targets.","defense":"The Saints defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 3rd and 20th. Cameron Jordan led the team with 12 sacks, and Marcus Davenport added 4.5 despite playing only 47% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd highest nickel usage in the league.","special":"The Saints were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 93% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 15% of the money on the books. Their receivers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Drew Brees played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,992 yards. Michael Thomas was the clear top target with 29% of the looks and 1,405 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Alvin Kamara and Mark Ingram. On defense, Cameron Jordan led the team with 12 sacks and Marcus Williams picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Sean Payton called the offense as head coach and Dennis Allen called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2018 Chargers and the 2025 Patriots under Josh McDaniels. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Colts under Lou Anarumo and the 2023 Chargers under Brandon Staley.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Saints changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}